<DocsLayout title="Orchestration" description="One scmle workflow definition for ingestion, training, evaluation, monitoring and batch prediction, and a local executor with durable state." current="orchestration">
  <p class="eyebrow"><span></span> Serve and operate</p>
  <h1>One workflow,<br /><em>defined once.</em></h1>
  <p class="lead">A workflow definition for ingestion, training, evaluation, monitoring and batch prediction; and a local executor that runs it with durable state.</p>

  <h2>The definition</h2>
  <p>A <code>Workflow</code> is a set of named, versioned <code>Step</code>s with explicit <code>depends_on</code>. A step has a kind (<code>ingest</code>, <code>train</code>, <code>evaluate</code>, <code>monitor</code>, <code>batch_predict</code> or <code>approval</code>), a timeout, a <code>Retries</code> (attempts, first delay, factor, ceiling), an optional cost budget and an optional concurrency group. The workflow adds an optional <code>Schedule</code>, <code>max_concurrent_runs</code>, a run cost budget, group limits and <code>max_parallel</code>. Dependency cycles, unknown dependencies and duplicate names are refused when the object is built.</p>
  <div class="callout"><p><strong>Automatic versus approved.</strong> A step with <code>effect="approved"</code> may only exist behind an <code>approval</code> step it depends on; the definition refuses otherwise. The standard workflow is: ingest, train, evaluate, then monitor and an approval in parallel, then batch prediction (approved).</p></div>

  <h2>The local executor</h2>
  <p>State lives in an <code>ObjectStore</code> (see <a href={`${base}/docs/governed-data/`}>Governed data</a>) under <code>workflows/&lt;name&gt;/runs/&lt;run id&gt;/</code>.</p>
  <DocTable label="Executor behaviour" head={["Concern", "Behaviour"]} rows={executor} />
  <p>A <code>Scheduler</code> turns the schedule into slots and applies the declared catch-up policy to missed ones: <code>run_all</code> (bounded by <code>max_catch_up</code>), <code>latest_only</code>, or <code>skip</code> (only a slot still within a tenth of an interval). Run ids come from the slot, so a scheduler that lost its state cannot repeat a finished slot. The first tick does not backfill. A slot that cannot start because the workflow is busy is <code>deferred</code> and stays due.</p>

  <h2>What was not done</h2>
  <p>The definition runs only on the local executor. The package emits no definition for an external workflow engine; concurrency limits, cost budgets and groups are enforced by the local executor only. The approval step is not wired to a notification channel or a UI.</p>

  <p class="meta">Workflow surface of the 0.1 beta line</p>
</DocsLayout>
